                <pb facs="0829" n="767" xml:id="pg767"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure to inclosure in No. 27.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Statement in regard to the tonnage-tax paid at Havana
                        in one year by American fishing smacks.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Havana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1877-11-02"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 2,
                        1877</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Tabular statement showing the names of 18 American vessels employed in the live
                    fish trade between Havana and Key West; the number of voyages made by each,
                    their tonnage as per register, and the same by Spanish admeasurement; the amount
                    of tonnage-tax paid by each in Havana during the year 1876; and the tax that
                    Spanish vessels of the same tonnage and making the same number of voyages would
                    have paid in the United States during the same period.</p>
                <p>Accompanying a slip from the New York Herald of 20th October, 1877, with a
                    telegram from Madrid respecting the tonnage-tax collected from Spanish vessels
                    in the United States.</p>
                <p>Accompanying, also, two original bills of consular fees paid by American vessels
                    to the Spanish consul at Key West.</p>
            </div>
